WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.849
[crickets chirping]

2
00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.523
[eerie music playing]

3
00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:40.331
[dogs barking]

4
00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:49.257
[car alarm blaring]

5
00:00:57.807 --> 00:01:01.644
- [eerie music intensifies]
- [girl] <i>Four, five...</i>

6
00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:06.149
[man 1] Well, the first time
that I thought about infinity,

7
00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:09.486
I was looking up at the sky one night

8
00:01:10.695 --> 00:01:12.530
when I was about ten years old.

9
00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:17.285
[woman 1] I was really little,
it was before I had started school,

10
00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:19.871
and I was sitting
under the dining room table

11
00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:22.874
and counting.

12
00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:26.294
And at a certain point,

13
00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:29.464
I realize this goes on forever.

14
00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:35.470
[man 2] Lying on a beach late at night
and just wondering

15
00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:37.764
the vastness of outer space.

16
00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:39.724
Does it go on forever?

17
00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:41.935
[tense music plays]

18
00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:00.703
[music intensifies]

19
00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:05.875
[tense music ends]

20
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.961
[jaunty jazz music plays]

21
00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.683
[cat] Mmm...

22
00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:24.286
[producer] I want to start
with sort of the premise of the film,

23
00:02:24.352 --> 00:02:26.312
which is that we're looking for infinity.

24
00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:29.190
- So where should we go looking?
- [chuckles]

25
00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:35.405
Well, I don't think
everybody's looking for infinity. [laughs]

26
00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.699
[upbeat music plays]

27
00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:41.035
[woman] So is infinity a number, a place,

28
00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:43.037
an idea, a concept?

29
00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:45.623
If you ask me, probably all of the above.

30
00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:55.675
[man 1] Numbers never end.
That's the basic idea of infinity.

31
00:02:56.217 --> 00:03:00.722
[man 2] If I imagine continuing to count
for as long as I can count,

32
00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:04.517
infinity will definitely last
longer than my lifetime.

33
00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.270
[man 3] The funny thing is,
all these numbers that you can imagine,

34
00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:09.522
they're like zero, nothing.

35
00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:11.316
Any number, no matter how big,

36
00:03:11.399 --> 00:03:15.278
is just absolutely insignificant
compared to infinity.

37
00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.030
For me, infinity is not scary.

38
00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:20.325
I find infinity beautiful,
and haunting, and thrilling.

39
00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:21.408
I love Infinity.

40
00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:25.205
You know, I guess if I start
thinking about, "I'll be dead forever..."

41
00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:29.792
there's a side of me
that's worried about it. [laughs]

42
00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.086
But then again,
I don't plan to be thinking about it.

43
00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:37.967
I think to be conscious
is to be wrangling with infinity.

44
00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:39.594
I mean, not to be grandiose.

45
00:03:39.677 --> 00:03:44.224
When our hearts are broken,
are we going to be in pain forever?

46
00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:46.351
And if you really
fall in love with someone,

47
00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.186
are you gonna be in love
with them forever?

48
00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:52.565
Love is certainly infinite,
because when we are overwhelmed by love,

49
00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:55.818
we have this constant sense
of breaking the limits and the boundaries.

50
00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:59.572
All of these abstractions,
suddenly it seemed to me,

51
00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:02.533
well, maybe they're just
the product of our minds.

52
00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:03.785
Who invented infinity?

53
00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:06.246
I think, uh, to some extent,

54
00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:09.540
the concept goes back
earlier than anything that we know.

55
00:04:09.624 --> 00:04:13.002
[woman] <i>One, two, three...</i>

56
00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:16.589
[Kenny] In fact, counting
is the very earliest writing that we have.

57
00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.675
[girl] <i>...seven...</i>

58
00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.928
[Kenny] Whoever invented writing
already had the idea of counting,

59
00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:25.056
and the idea
that you can always count one more

60
00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:27.392
must surely go back at least that far.

61
00:04:27.892 --> 00:04:29.560
[girl] <i>...fifteen, sixteen...</i>

62
00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:33.064
[Steven] We have all kinds of different
visions of things that are infinite,

63
00:04:33.147 --> 00:04:34.482
and they're usually terrifying.

64
00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:37.694
There's the vision of the bottomless pit.

65
00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.239
There's the idea of time that never ends.

66
00:04:41.322 --> 00:04:43.825
[clock ticking]

67
00:04:43.908 --> 00:04:45.159
If you believe in hell,

68
00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:48.663
and if you believe
about torture going on forever,

69
00:04:48.746 --> 00:04:51.249
a nightmare that you could
never wake up from...

70
00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:58.172
So I understand that a lot of people
find thinking about infinity terrifying,

71
00:04:58.256 --> 00:04:59.716
or nauseating.

72
00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.301
At least very upsetting.

73
00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:07.265
[Rebecca] But for some of us,
it's one of the deepest pleasures.

74
00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:08.891
[girl] <i>...2001...</i>

75
00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.643
[Rebecca] We're so small...

76
00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:12.603
[girl] <i>...2003, 2004...</i>

77
00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:15.398
[Rebecca] ...and yet we can
touch something so...

78
00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.275
[girl] <i>...1000000, 10000002...</i>

79
00:05:17.358 --> 00:05:19.319
[Rebecca] ...explosively large.

80
00:05:19.402 --> 00:05:21.321
[girl] <i>...1000004...</i>

81
00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:27.035
[Rebecca] That feeling of, "I'm bigger
because I know how small I am."

82
00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:29.787
[girl] <i>...399, 400, 401...</i>

83
00:05:29.871 --> 00:05:31.956
[Rebecca] I've been chasing that feeling

84
00:05:33.541 --> 00:05:35.293
my entire life.

85
00:05:35.376 --> 00:05:37.378
[girl continues counting]

86
00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:44.844
[Janna] But does infinity exist
outside of our minds?

87
00:05:46.262 --> 00:05:48.264
[Moon] So does infinity exist?

88
00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:50.058
Um...

89
00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.021
You know, in math, I think... [smacks lips]

90
00:05:55.104 --> 00:05:57.231
[producer] Can we wait
for this train to go by?

91
00:05:57.315 --> 00:05:58.316
Oh yeah.

92
00:05:58.399 --> 00:06:00.860
[train rattling]

93
00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:08.533
[producer] Okay.

94
00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:10.536
All right. Sorry about that.

95
00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.997
Oh, yeah. It's good to collect thoughts.

96
00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:17.418
Um, okay, so I think
I was gonna make a comment

97
00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:20.171
about what it means
to exist mathematically, right?

98
00:06:20.254 --> 00:06:26.928
Um, so, in math, I think we have
an interesting relationship to stuff,

99
00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:28.846
to things in existence, right?

100
00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:31.933
If you can conceive of something,

101
00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:34.977
if you can kind of
create rules for handling it,

102
00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:36.312
then it exists.

103
00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:38.439
[train rattling]

104
00:06:38.523 --> 00:06:40.983
So how does infinity fit into all that?

105
00:06:42.944 --> 00:06:46.322
Well, okay, infinity
is super counterintuitive,

106
00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:48.950
and it leads
to a lot of surprising paradoxes,

107
00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:51.661
contradictions,
intellectual quagmires, quicksand.

108
00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:54.038
Many of these are summed up in a parable

109
00:06:54.122 --> 00:06:56.499
that goes by the name
of the Infinite Hotel.

110
00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.584
[jaunty jazz music plays]

111
00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:02.713
Imagine a hotel
with infinitely many rooms.

112
00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:06.801
It's a really popular hotel.

113
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:11.013
And in fact, it's always booked solid.
Every room is occupied.

114
00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:14.475
Nevertheless, there's always room
at the Infinite Hotel.

115
00:07:15.226 --> 00:07:16.227
[Steven laughs]

116
00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.856
So, one night, a new guest shows up.

117
00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:22.066
- [bell dinging]
- Bang, bang, bang on the bell.

118
00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:23.359
"I'd like a room."

119
00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:25.403
And the manager says,

120
00:07:25.486 --> 00:07:28.865
"Sure. Of course, we can accommodate you
here at the Infinite Hotel."

121
00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:30.741
"Just hold on for one minute."

122
00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:34.162
The manager tells everyone
over the loudspeaker...

123
00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:37.248
[manager] <i>Please gather your things.</i>

124
00:07:37.331 --> 00:07:40.918
<i>Get ready to move
to the next room down the hall.</i>

125
00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:43.463
[Steven] Person in room one
moves into room two.

126
00:07:43.546 --> 00:07:46.466
Person in room two moves into room three.

127
00:07:46.549 --> 00:07:50.511
You might think there's gonna be trouble
because all the rooms are occupied.

128
00:07:50.595 --> 00:07:53.681
Nevertheless, as it's an Infinite Hotel,
I mean, this can happen, right?

129
00:07:53.764 --> 00:07:55.266
The numbers never stop.

130
00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:57.351
One can move to two,
two can move to three,

131
00:07:57.435 --> 00:07:59.770
a million can move to a million and one.

132
00:07:59.854 --> 00:08:02.940
So everybody moves down to the next room,

133
00:08:03.024 --> 00:08:07.445
which means that now room one is open,
and the new guest can be accommodated.

134
00:08:08.029 --> 00:08:10.114
[producer] You could keep everyone
in the same room

135
00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:11.991
and just have the guest go to the end.

136
00:08:12.074 --> 00:08:16.329
Yeah. So a tempting thought would be
just have the guest take the last room.

137
00:08:16.412 --> 00:08:19.665
[laughs] Why are we making
everybody else move for this one person?

138
00:08:19.749 --> 00:08:21.501
Well, because there is no last room.

139
00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:23.085
[jaunty jazz music plays]

140
00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:24.879
Infinity doesn't work like that.

141
00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:28.758
You have to start at the beginning,
not at the end. There is no end.

142
00:08:30.259 --> 00:08:33.513
Now, a bigger challenge for the manager
comes the next night

143
00:08:33.596 --> 00:08:36.557
when, instead of just one guest
showing up,

144
00:08:37.475 --> 00:08:42.271
suddenly a whole busload full of cranky,
sweaty, ill-tempered people

145
00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:47.860
all pound on the bell at the same time,
saying, "We all want rooms."

146
00:08:47.944 --> 00:08:49.695
There's infinitely many new guests.

147
00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:51.989
They all want to be accommodated
at this hotel.

148
00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:54.533
How can you do that?

149
00:08:54.575 --> 00:08:57.620
Can you somehow find room
for infinitely many new people,

150
00:08:57.703 --> 00:09:00.206
given that infinitely
many rooms are occupied?

151
00:09:00.289 --> 00:09:02.291
[panting]

152
00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:07.421
It turns out the manager has seen
this problem before,

153
00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:09.715
and so she calls out over the loudspeaker...

154
00:09:09.799 --> 00:09:12.635
[manager] <i>Everyone be prepared
in a few minutes</i>

155
00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:15.846
<i>to move to the room
that is double your current room.</i>

156
00:09:16.764 --> 00:09:19.392
[Steven] So, person in room one,
you're gonna be in room two.

157
00:09:19.475 --> 00:09:22.937
Person in room two, you're now
going to be moving into room four.

158
00:09:23.646 --> 00:09:26.148
Person in room three, you're going to six.

159
00:09:27.358 --> 00:09:30.111
Now, it's a big inconvenience
for person in room one million.

160
00:09:30.194 --> 00:09:33.781
They have to move to room two million,
which is a big schlep down the hall.

161
00:09:33.864 --> 00:09:35.616
[jaunty jazz music intensifies]

162
00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.828
So all these people dutifully move
to their new rooms.

163
00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:41.497
You'll notice that what's happened is
all the odd-numbered rooms

164
00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:42.581
have been vacated.

165
00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:45.544
And so all those new guests
can just start filing in.

166
00:09:46.127 --> 00:09:47.336
[jaunty jazz music ends]

167
00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.006
There's one other custom
at the Infinite Hotel,

168
00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:53.175
which is that this manager
is very conscientious

169
00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:56.721
and always checks in on the rooms,
infinitely many of them.

170
00:09:58.347 --> 00:10:00.182
Fortunately, the manager is very speedy,

171
00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:02.935
so she can get her whole job done
in a minute.

172
00:10:03.019 --> 00:10:04.979
- [jaunty jazz music plays]
- [grunts]

173
00:10:05.062 --> 00:10:08.232
She spends half a minute
making sure everything's okay in room one,

174
00:10:08.858 --> 00:10:14.071
and a quarter of a minute,
so 15 seconds, on room two,

175
00:10:14.155 --> 00:10:18.743
and then half of that,
so 7.5 seconds, on room three,

176
00:10:18.826 --> 00:10:22.038
and so on, going all the way down the hall
in the Infinite Hotel.

177
00:10:23.164 --> 00:10:27.293
A half, plus a quarter,
plus an eighth, plus a sixteenth,

178
00:10:27.376 --> 00:10:28.419
and if you add that up...

179
00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:33.299
But if you just think about that,
when you go out to infinity,

180
00:10:33.883 --> 00:10:35.509
that's gonna add up to one.

181
00:10:38.721 --> 00:10:40.598
She's basically gonna be done

182
00:10:40.681 --> 00:10:44.727
checking infinitely many rooms
in one minute.

183
00:10:45.269 --> 00:10:47.109
[producer] How does she get back
from infinity?

184
00:10:47.188 --> 00:10:50.775
She get... Oh, that's good. Well... [laughs]

185
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.445
Yeah, let's see. So, um... Hmm.

186
00:10:55.196 --> 00:10:59.825
That's an interesting puzzle.
Yeah. Where is she at the end? Uh...

187
00:11:00.743 --> 00:11:01.743
Don't ask me.

188
00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:05.122
I've been thinking about infinity
my whole career,

189
00:11:05.206 --> 00:11:07.500
and even I don't really see
any way out for her.

190
00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:09.585
[eerie music plays]

191
00:11:10.795 --> 00:11:13.673
She's out at the end
of this infinitely long hallway,

192
00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:16.592
and I don't see
how she's gonna come back from there.

193
00:11:19.762 --> 00:11:21.555
I told you it was a strange hotel.

194
00:11:23.974 --> 00:11:27.186
Well, so I think that, you know,
the moral of this crazy parable

195
00:11:27.269 --> 00:11:30.398
is that infinity doesn't behave
like anything we're used to.

196
00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:36.112
We're used to thinking about
collections of finite numbers of things.

197
00:11:36.195 --> 00:11:38.948
Fish, fish, fish.
That's three fish, you know.

198
00:11:39.031 --> 00:11:41.617
So we're used to small numbers,
or even big numbers.

199
00:11:41.701 --> 00:11:43.953
You hear nowadays
about trillions of dollars

200
00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:46.956
needing to be spent
on the budget in the United States.

201
00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:52.128
But, um, those are nothing like infinity.

202
00:11:52.211 --> 00:11:55.881
Any finite number, no matter how big,
is nothing compared to infinity.

203
00:11:55.965 --> 00:11:58.426
[marching drum playing]

204
00:11:59.051 --> 00:12:02.221
We just don't have good intuition
about how infinite things work.

205
00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.182
[tense music plays]

206
00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:13.899
Even very small children
can have a gut feeling about infinity.

207
00:12:13.983 --> 00:12:15.276
It's the biggest possible thing.

208
00:12:15.359 --> 00:12:17.611
It's bigger
than anything you can ever think of.

209
00:12:21.157 --> 00:12:22.867
Well, what happens if you add one?

210
00:12:24.702 --> 00:12:26.704
So if infinity
is the biggest thing there is,

211
00:12:26.787 --> 00:12:30.750
then when you add one,
it should still be infinity. Maybe?

212
00:12:31.792 --> 00:12:35.087
What happens if I subtract infinity
from both sides?

213
00:12:35.671 --> 00:12:38.299
Then you get one equals zero.

214
00:12:38.382 --> 00:12:41.635
And so immediately you've landed yourself
in some kind of an issue,

215
00:12:41.719 --> 00:12:43.220
and that's just the start.

216
00:12:43.304 --> 00:12:46.265
It turns out you can write things down
that don't have an answer.

217
00:12:46.766 --> 00:12:49.018
I mean, that doesn't happen
in finite arithmetic.

218
00:12:49.101 --> 00:12:52.104
If I say one plus two,
the answer is three, and I'm done with it.

219
00:12:52.855 --> 00:12:55.566
But if I say one minus one,
plus one, minus one...

220
00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:56.817
[voice echoes]

221
00:12:56.901 --> 00:13:00.863
One minus one, plus one, minus one,
plus one, minus one, dot, dot, dot.

222
00:13:00.946 --> 00:13:02.490
Keep doing that forever.

223
00:13:05.284 --> 00:13:08.370
There is no answer to that.
It's not zero and it's not one.

224
00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:10.915
It's neither. It's both. What is it?

225
00:13:10.998 --> 00:13:14.335
You can try adding infinity to infinity.
Multiply it.

226
00:13:14.418 --> 00:13:15.961
You get these weird paradoxes.

227
00:13:16.045 --> 00:13:18.172
Well, what's infinity plus infinity?

228
00:13:18.255 --> 00:13:20.966
That should also be infinity
because it's the biggest thing.

229
00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:23.677
Then you subtract infinity from both sides

230
00:13:23.761 --> 00:13:25.638
and you get infinity equals zero.

231
00:13:25.721 --> 00:13:27.973
That's even worse. So this is terrible.

232
00:13:28.057 --> 00:13:30.559
[tense music continues]

233
00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:33.979
But it's wonderful,
because what is going on?

234
00:13:34.063 --> 00:13:36.065
[waves crashing]

235
00:13:38.567 --> 00:13:41.737
Is infinity beautiful?
Of course, it's beautiful.

236
00:13:41.821 --> 00:13:43.322
I mean, yeah, it's...

237
00:13:43.405 --> 00:13:46.659
Like I say, it's beautiful because it is...

238
00:13:47.243 --> 00:13:48.243
Uh...

239
00:13:49.078 --> 00:13:50.162
Maybe...

240
00:13:51.539 --> 00:13:53.833
a good way to think about it
is with our circle.

241
00:13:53.916 --> 00:13:55.334
Shall we talk about the circle?

242
00:13:55.417 --> 00:13:57.419
[eerie music plays]

243
00:14:02.383 --> 00:14:04.009
[Steven] There's a very beautiful shape

244
00:14:04.093 --> 00:14:07.221
that often people think of
as the most perfect shape, a circle.

245
00:14:09.765 --> 00:14:12.351
Perfectly round, never-ending.

246
00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:15.187
We see circles everywhere.

247
00:14:16.105 --> 00:14:18.732
I look in your eyes,
I see the circle of your iris.

248
00:14:18.816 --> 00:14:20.401
I see the circle of your pupil.

249
00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:23.863
I see the circle of the sun and the moon.

250
00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:26.782
I've got my wedding ring
in the shape of a circle.

251
00:14:27.867 --> 00:14:28.993
[Eugenia] I love circles.

252
00:14:29.618 --> 00:14:32.162
But if you sit down and think about
what they are for a second,

253
00:14:32.246 --> 00:14:36.250
how many sides do they have, for example?
How many corners do they have?

254
00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:39.420
[Steven] That immediately brings us
into face-to-face confrontation

255
00:14:39.503 --> 00:14:40.754
with the infinite,

256
00:14:41.297 --> 00:14:44.425
because you can't think about a circle
in terms of straight lines.

257
00:14:45.676 --> 00:14:48.470
[Eugenia] If we think about pointy shapes,
say a triangle,

258
00:14:49.096 --> 00:14:50.848
if we tried to use it as a wheel,

259
00:14:50.931 --> 00:14:53.726
it would be kind of painful
going up the street.

260
00:14:55.311 --> 00:14:59.690
If we had ten corners,
then that wouldn't be so bad as a wheel.

261
00:15:00.441 --> 00:15:03.027
And then, if you keep going,
you can go, "Oh, wait,

262
00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:06.113
if we then had infinity sides,

263
00:15:06.196 --> 00:15:09.116
then we would be completely like a circle,

264
00:15:09.199 --> 00:15:13.579
and we would have infinity corners,
which is like having no corners."

265
00:15:13.662 --> 00:15:17.958
And so, somehow, infinity has come round
to being just like zero.

266
00:15:18.042 --> 00:15:19.042
[bell rings]

267
00:15:24.006 --> 00:15:26.592
One of the amazing things about infinity

268
00:15:26.675 --> 00:15:30.721
is that, if you scale it,
it's still infinity.

269
00:15:30.804 --> 00:15:33.557
Whereas if you take any finite number,
if you scale it,

270
00:15:33.641 --> 00:15:36.268
which is like multiplying it,
it gets bigger.

271
00:15:36.352 --> 00:15:38.812
But infinity doesn't do that somehow,

272
00:15:38.896 --> 00:15:42.149
and that's a very odd thing
for us to get our heads around.

273
00:15:44.109 --> 00:15:45.861
So let's try and picture this, right?

274
00:15:46.487 --> 00:15:51.033
When it comes to a circle
as a set of points...

275
00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:53.994
'Cause that's what it is.
It's an infinite collection of points.

276
00:15:54.495 --> 00:15:56.705
I would like to try to convince you

277
00:15:56.789 --> 00:16:00.042
that a small circle,
like a circle of radius one,

278
00:16:00.751 --> 00:16:04.380
um, has the same number of points
as a huge circle,

279
00:16:04.463 --> 00:16:05.798
a circle of radius billion.

280
00:16:06.382 --> 00:16:07.508
Right? Okay.

281
00:16:07.591 --> 00:16:09.093
So, to do that,

282
00:16:09.176 --> 00:16:12.429
I need to get you to agree
on what it means to be the same size.

283
00:16:12.513 --> 00:16:15.432
Like, anytime you're making
a high-stakes bet with a friend,

284
00:16:15.516 --> 00:16:17.726
you wanna settle...
This is good advice, by the way.

285
00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:21.063
You want to settle in advance
what qualifies as winning.

286
00:16:23.482 --> 00:16:27.444
I propose that two sets
will be said to have the same size

287
00:16:27.528 --> 00:16:30.781
if I can put their elements
in a perfect one-to-one correspondence.

288
00:16:32.533 --> 00:16:36.912
Now, all I need to give you is a rule
that puts the points of a small circle

289
00:16:36.996 --> 00:16:39.498
in correspondence
with the points of a larger circle.

290
00:16:39.581 --> 00:16:42.042
You can't count the points on the circle,

291
00:16:42.126 --> 00:16:45.713
but you can match them up
with the points on a bigger circle.

292
00:16:45.796 --> 00:16:47.131
I take my small circle

293
00:16:47.631 --> 00:16:50.426
and I just center it somewhere at a point,

294
00:16:50.509 --> 00:16:53.470
and then I take the huge circle
and I center it at the same point.

295
00:16:53.971 --> 00:16:58.892
I'm gonna... From that center point,
I'm gonna draw all the radii. Right?

296
00:16:58.976 --> 00:17:01.437
I'll draw these kind of line segments

297
00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.023
stretching out from the center
to the outer circle.

298
00:17:04.732 --> 00:17:08.152
Well, each of those line segments
hits the outer circle on one point

299
00:17:08.736 --> 00:17:12.072
and hits the inner circle on one point,
and that's the correspondence.

300
00:17:12.156 --> 00:17:16.452
I'm gonna say the point, um,
on a particular radius in the small circle

301
00:17:16.535 --> 00:17:20.164
is matched to its corresponding point
from the large circle.

302
00:17:20.706 --> 00:17:23.292
Well, by sweeping those radii
all the way around,

303
00:17:23.375 --> 00:17:26.879
I clearly hit
all the points on each circle.

304
00:17:26.962 --> 00:17:28.380
And so I'm done.

305
00:17:29.631 --> 00:17:32.092
I've got a perfect
one-to-one correspondence.

306
00:17:32.176 --> 00:17:33.677
You now have to agree

307
00:17:33.761 --> 00:17:36.221
that the two infinite sets
have the same size.

308
00:17:36.305 --> 00:17:38.724
[upbeat music plays]

309
00:17:38.807 --> 00:17:43.604
So mathematicians don't literally
sit there counting to infinity.

310
00:17:43.687 --> 00:17:46.440
We just match things up in pairs.

311
00:17:46.523 --> 00:17:48.942
And if you can find a way
to match things up in pairs,

312
00:17:49.026 --> 00:17:51.070
you can go, "Oh, that's the same then."

313
00:17:51.153 --> 00:17:55.157
So, for example, we can match up
all the numbers, the whole numbers,

314
00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.244
with the even numbers
by just multiplying each one by two.

315
00:17:59.328 --> 00:18:02.539
You sit there and go, "Wait,
the even numbers is only half of them."

316
00:18:02.623 --> 00:18:04.958
And then you go,
"But wait, I can match them up,

317
00:18:05.042 --> 00:18:07.753
because if I pair up one with two,

318
00:18:07.836 --> 00:18:09.379
and two with four,

319
00:18:09.463 --> 00:18:10.756
and three with six,

320
00:18:10.839 --> 00:18:12.800
then they pair up exactly."

321
00:18:12.883 --> 00:18:15.594
So then you go,
"Wait, there's the same number,

322
00:18:15.677 --> 00:18:17.262
even though it's half of them."

323
00:18:17.346 --> 00:18:20.933
And that's the amazing thing
about infinity.

324
00:18:21.892 --> 00:18:24.436
Once you've figured out
this way of matching things up

325
00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:26.855
to see how big an infinite set is,

326
00:18:26.939 --> 00:18:30.609
it turns out that there might be some
that you can never match up,

327
00:18:30.692 --> 00:18:34.238
which means that there is
some kind of bigger possible infinity.

328
00:18:34.321 --> 00:18:36.323
[tense music plays]

329
00:18:38.909 --> 00:18:40.589
[Moon] How can there be larger infinities?

330
00:18:40.619 --> 00:18:43.997
Infinity is already
as big as we can imagine, right?

331
00:18:44.081 --> 00:18:45.624
No. No.

332
00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.836
[hesitates] There's something beyond that.

333
00:18:50.379 --> 00:18:52.482
- Let me see if this works for you.
- [producer] Okay.

334
00:18:52.506 --> 00:18:55.134
Um, so, provably the smallest infinity

335
00:18:55.217 --> 00:18:58.011
is the one that goes
one, two, three, dot, dot, dot.

336
00:18:58.512 --> 00:19:01.390
If I was to take the numbers zero and one

337
00:19:01.473 --> 00:19:04.143
and ask you
how many numbers are there between that,

338
00:19:04.226 --> 00:19:06.478
well, you could keep
dividing and dividing.

339
00:19:06.562 --> 00:19:08.355
No matter how many times you divide,

340
00:19:09.273 --> 00:19:10.691
there's always a number

341
00:19:10.774 --> 00:19:14.736
that you could add another zero
behind the last decimal you had put.

342
00:19:15.863 --> 00:19:18.657
[Eugenia] And I can make
a one-to-one little map,

343
00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:22.202
like a little dictionary between
those numbers and the whole numbers.

344
00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:24.079
One, two, three, four, five.

345
00:19:26.832 --> 00:19:31.295
And so what we say is that
those two infinite sets are the same size.

346
00:19:34.464 --> 00:19:36.425
But then there's also other numbers.

347
00:19:36.508 --> 00:19:38.719
Things like the square root of two,

348
00:19:38.802 --> 00:19:41.054
and pi, and E.

349
00:19:41.138 --> 00:19:43.807
And those are just a few
that we know about and use.

350
00:19:44.892 --> 00:19:47.477
These are numbers
that aren't ordinary fractions,

351
00:19:47.561 --> 00:19:49.646
and if we try
and write them down as decimals,

352
00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:53.734
they'll somehow go on forever
without ever repeating themselves.

353
00:19:55.402 --> 00:19:57.696
So how can we ever say what they are?

354
00:19:58.405 --> 00:20:02.826
It took mathematicians hundreds of years
to figure out how to do it rigorously,

355
00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:06.330
and when they did,
they realized that they're so complicated,

356
00:20:06.413 --> 00:20:10.125
it's actually impossible
to put them in a list.

357
00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:12.294
No matter how we try to list them,

358
00:20:12.377 --> 00:20:15.964
we're doomed to miss some out
along the way.

359
00:20:16.673 --> 00:20:19.301
We can't make a one-to-one correspondence

360
00:20:19.384 --> 00:20:22.221
between those numbers
and the counting numbers.

361
00:20:26.683 --> 00:20:28.685
Now that we have two infinities,

362
00:20:28.769 --> 00:20:30.604
would we really stop there?

363
00:20:31.897 --> 00:20:33.897
[Moon] There are bigger and bigger
and bigger ones.

364
00:20:34.358 --> 00:20:37.778
This is a trail that just keeps on going.

365
00:20:37.861 --> 00:20:39.863
[eerie music plays]

366
00:20:42.115 --> 00:20:45.994
[Eugenia]
An infinite hierarchy of infinities.

367
00:20:48.956 --> 00:20:50.415
This is the kind of thing

368
00:20:50.499 --> 00:20:53.460
that, hopefully, turns some people
into mathematicians.

369
00:20:53.961 --> 00:20:57.547
And no doubt sends other people running
in the other direction. [laughs]

370
00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:02.761
[Steven] My wife gets nauseous
when I bring up infinity.

371
00:21:04.513 --> 00:21:06.765
And my kids don't want
to hear about it either.

372
00:21:09.184 --> 00:21:11.436
[Eugenia] Some people love
peering over cliff edges.

373
00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:13.605
There are now those glass bridges

374
00:21:13.689 --> 00:21:16.149
where you can step out
over the Grand Canyon.

375
00:21:16.233 --> 00:21:18.485
There's no way I would do that.

376
00:21:18.568 --> 00:21:22.823
But when it's a mathematical cliff edge,
I love it.

377
00:21:24.241 --> 00:21:27.494
I think this gets back
to this concept of the sublime.

378
00:21:30.038 --> 00:21:32.249
When you're out by a waterfall...

379
00:21:32.332 --> 00:21:34.876
[water running]

380
00:21:37.254 --> 00:21:40.507
...you have this feeling of this thing
that is so much larger than yourself.

381
00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:44.761
But then you climb onto the mountain peak
and you look out at the next valley,

382
00:21:44.845 --> 00:21:47.306
and you see another waterfall
that's even larger,

383
00:21:47.389 --> 00:21:49.308
but it's in the distance,
so it looks tiny.

384
00:21:49.391 --> 00:21:51.393
[birds singing]

385
00:21:55.397 --> 00:21:58.984
Infinity is some kind of monster
that has to be tamed.

386
00:21:59.067 --> 00:22:01.361
[violin music plays]

387
00:22:03.322 --> 00:22:07.534
"INFINITY"

388
00:22:13.915 --> 00:22:15.667
[loud thump]

389
00:22:17.377 --> 00:22:19.129
[roars]

390
00:22:20.797 --> 00:22:24.509
[roars, grunts]

391
00:22:27.846 --> 00:22:30.057
[alarm blaring]

392
00:22:31.683 --> 00:22:35.187
[in Japanese]
It's infinity! It's attacking the city!

393
00:22:35.979 --> 00:22:39.566
[Moon in English] Mathematicians needed
to invent ways to deal with infinity.

394
00:22:39.649 --> 00:22:42.652
Taking something totally weird
and unintuitive

395
00:22:42.736 --> 00:22:45.072
and taming it to the point where you can

396
00:22:45.155 --> 00:22:47.282
walk around and study it from all sides.

397
00:22:47.366 --> 00:22:49.951
[roars]

398
00:22:54.831 --> 00:22:56.124
[grunts]

399
00:23:01.171 --> 00:23:06.259
And the ways that they came up with
led to the field of rigorous calculus.

400
00:23:06.343 --> 00:23:08.428
There's one idea at the heart of calculus,

401
00:23:08.512 --> 00:23:11.515
and it's an idea
I like to call the infinity principle.

402
00:23:11.598 --> 00:23:14.434
[upbeat music plays]

403
00:23:15.018 --> 00:23:20.732
[Steven] You can make sense
of any complicated motion or phenomenon,

404
00:23:20.816 --> 00:23:23.777
or anything that's changing,
or any curved shape,

405
00:23:24.528 --> 00:23:28.573
by thinking of it
as being made of an infinite number

406
00:23:28.657 --> 00:23:33.370
of infinitesimally small simpler motions
or shapes.

407
00:23:35.497 --> 00:23:37.666
This is one of the greatest ideas
in history.

408
00:23:38.375 --> 00:23:40.502
[monster roars]

409
00:23:40.585 --> 00:23:43.880
Everything to do with movement,

410
00:23:43.964 --> 00:23:47.259
and everything to do with things
that are continuously changing,

411
00:23:47.342 --> 00:23:50.137
can only be studied rigorously
using calculus.

412
00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:51.513
[roars]

413
00:23:51.596 --> 00:23:53.348
[woman] Hmm...

414
00:23:53.432 --> 00:23:58.437
[in Japanese] Maybe it's trying
to communicate with us!

415
00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:02.441
We can use calculus to study its roar!

416
00:24:02.524 --> 00:24:04.404
[Steven in English]
So the infinity principle is

417
00:24:04.443 --> 00:24:06.153
you can make sense of complicated things

418
00:24:06.236 --> 00:24:09.990
by breaking them up
into infinitely many simpler things.

419
00:24:11.783 --> 00:24:13.076
[graphic beeps]

420
00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.454
Solve the problem for the simpler things

421
00:24:17.038 --> 00:24:20.834
and then add the results back together
to get the original whole.

422
00:24:20.917 --> 00:24:22.919
[upbeat music continues]

423
00:24:33.638 --> 00:24:36.975
[in Japanese]
A picture encoded in the roar!

424
00:24:37.058 --> 00:24:39.436
Stop fighting infinity!

425
00:24:39.519 --> 00:24:42.105
It's peaceful. Look!

426
00:24:43.273 --> 00:24:44.691
[electrical sparking]

427
00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:48.195
[Eugenia in English]
The understanding of electricity

428
00:24:48.278 --> 00:24:49.988
was made possible by calculus.

429
00:24:50.071 --> 00:24:51.071
[loud thump]

430
00:24:52.657 --> 00:24:54.784
[roars]

431
00:24:54.868 --> 00:24:58.205
And electricity has enabled
the entire modern world.

432
00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:00.081
[phone dials]

433
00:25:00.165 --> 00:25:02.042
<i>Moshi moshi!</i>

434
00:25:02.125 --> 00:25:05.295
[talks indistinctly]

435
00:25:05.378 --> 00:25:07.130
So does infinity exist?

436
00:25:07.214 --> 00:25:10.258
Well, in one of the senses of math,

437
00:25:10.342 --> 00:25:14.346
absolutely no question about it,
because we have a symbol for it,

438
00:25:14.429 --> 00:25:16.264
we know how to manipulate that symbol,

439
00:25:16.348 --> 00:25:19.643
we can agree on the conclusions
that we reach when doing so.

440
00:25:20.268 --> 00:25:23.855
And by doing so,
we can solve practical questions.

441
00:25:24.523 --> 00:25:28.401
Right? So, from a pure math point of view,

442
00:25:28.485 --> 00:25:30.904
that's your certificate of existence
right there.

443
00:25:35.158 --> 00:25:38.119
So then, does infinity exist out there?

444
00:25:39.871 --> 00:25:41.540
[upbeat music ends]

445
00:25:41.623 --> 00:25:44.042
That's above my pay grade. [laughs]

446
00:25:45.377 --> 00:25:46.711
[loud thump]

447
00:25:47.587 --> 00:25:50.048
If you mean does it physically exist,

448
00:25:51.383 --> 00:25:52.299
who knows?

449
00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:54.762
That's a question
for the physicists to answer.

450
00:25:56.638 --> 00:26:00.350
And so now they've got to go
do their expensive things, right?

451
00:26:00.433 --> 00:26:06.565
And figure out whether space-time
is infinite or not. It's a real question.

452
00:26:06.648 --> 00:26:08.650
[mysterious music plays]

453
00:26:12.237 --> 00:26:16.324
Oh, this is a great question.
So where can one look for real infinities?

454
00:26:17.951 --> 00:26:20.120
[Brian] We've been struggling
with the question

455
00:26:20.203 --> 00:26:25.709
of whether the infinite infinity
is a real thing

456
00:26:25.792 --> 00:26:30.463
or something that is a human invention
for a very long time.

457
00:26:31.339 --> 00:26:33.425
[Stephon]
Maybe we could approach the infinite.

458
00:26:33.925 --> 00:26:36.428
As a physicist,
that's the business I'm in.

459
00:26:37.512 --> 00:26:41.308
[Anthony] How thrilling would that be,
to do something in the physical world

460
00:26:41.391 --> 00:26:44.227
that would tell us
that something is physically infinite?

461
00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:49.316
[Carlo]
I think all of us have this experience

462
00:26:49.399 --> 00:26:55.155
of, you know, going out in the night
in the starry sky.

463
00:26:55.238 --> 00:26:57.907
[Stephon] Lying on a beach
late at night in Trinidad.

464
00:26:57.991 --> 00:27:00.035
I mean, I probably was, like,
eight years old

465
00:27:00.535 --> 00:27:04.039
and just wondering
the vastness of outer space.

466
00:27:07.834 --> 00:27:09.169
Does it go on forever?

467
00:27:13.423 --> 00:27:17.385
I question the notion of infinity

468
00:27:18.136 --> 00:27:23.099
when I am using a particular yardstick

469
00:27:23.183 --> 00:27:25.518
which is, can we measure it?

470
00:27:29.773 --> 00:27:33.401
Can we access it? [voice echoes]

471
00:27:33.485 --> 00:27:36.196
Can we, in any point

472
00:27:36.279 --> 00:27:39.991
wrap our arms around it
and say, "Here it is."

473
00:27:40.575 --> 00:27:41.951
"There's the infinite."

474
00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:47.666
And using that particular yardstick,
the answer is no.

475
00:27:47.749 --> 00:27:52.712
There's simply no way
that we can measure the infinite.

476
00:27:52.796 --> 00:27:58.259
[Janna] Nobody will ever give you back pi
to all of its infinite digits

477
00:27:58.343 --> 00:28:00.720
as the result of a measurement.

478
00:28:02.889 --> 00:28:06.559
All I'll ever measure, um,
in a laboratory,

479
00:28:06.643 --> 00:28:09.062
or my friends
will measure in laboratories,

480
00:28:09.145 --> 00:28:13.108
are approximations of infinite numbers.

481
00:28:15.819 --> 00:28:19.989
But then you start to wonder,
"Maybe these numbers don't really exist."

482
00:28:20.073 --> 00:28:23.201
"And nature doesn't actually
make use of them."

483
00:28:24.035 --> 00:28:25.120
And, um...

484
00:28:26.329 --> 00:28:28.456
And I don't know
the answer to that question.

485
00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:31.418
That's why sometimes you think,
"Oh, when the universe was created,

486
00:28:31.501 --> 00:28:35.964
maybe the speed
at which it came out expanding

487
00:28:36.047 --> 00:28:38.299
is an irrational number,

488
00:28:38.383 --> 00:28:43.513
like 0.500187923... [voice echoes]

489
00:28:43.596 --> 00:28:47.475
...and the universe is going to compute
that infinite list of digits

490
00:28:47.559 --> 00:28:49.227
over the course of time."

491
00:28:50.145 --> 00:28:54.399
So the universe itself
is performing a computation,

492
00:28:54.482 --> 00:28:57.527
and it's computing
infinite numbers possibly.

493
00:28:57.610 --> 00:29:02.073
[voices echoing]
<i>Five, zero, one, seven, eight...</i>

494
00:29:04.617 --> 00:29:07.036
[voices echoing indistinctly]

495
00:29:11.166 --> 00:29:12.792
[voices stop]

496
00:29:14.669 --> 00:29:16.212
I feel like I'm freaking you out,

497
00:29:16.296 --> 00:29:18.923
but, you know, Steven Strogatz had
to be pretty out there.

498
00:29:19.007 --> 00:29:20.216
- [producer] Oh.
- [laughs]

499
00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:21.509
- Oh yeah.
- [laughs]

500
00:29:21.593 --> 00:29:23.011
You think of a piece of Jell-O.

501
00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:27.640
You know, a blob, a block of Jell-O

502
00:29:27.724 --> 00:29:29.893
jiggling on the table or on a plate.

503
00:29:32.937 --> 00:29:34.689
You don't picture it as made of atoms.

504
00:29:34.773 --> 00:29:38.067
You think it's a continuum of Jell-O stuff

505
00:29:38.151 --> 00:29:40.612
that is infinitely subdivisible
and has no gaps.

506
00:29:40.695 --> 00:29:42.155
It hangs together perfectly.

507
00:29:42.238 --> 00:29:44.866
It's what most of us think
stuff really is like.

508
00:29:44.949 --> 00:29:46.868
[eerie music plays]

509
00:29:51.998 --> 00:29:54.793
The mathematicians are great
in talking about the continuum.

510
00:29:56.753 --> 00:30:01.716
And continuity is the idea
that if I take a line, just a short line,

511
00:30:02.258 --> 00:30:04.093
I can cut it in half,

512
00:30:05.178 --> 00:30:08.306
and then in half again,
and then in half again, and in half again.

513
00:30:10.391 --> 00:30:13.394
And I always have a line,
and I never stop.

514
00:30:13.478 --> 00:30:14.479
I can go forever.

515
00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:18.942
But a completely different question

516
00:30:19.025 --> 00:30:21.694
is whether things
are actually continuous in reality.

517
00:30:24.280 --> 00:30:26.157
Take a rope, cut it in half,

518
00:30:27.408 --> 00:30:29.869
and then again in half,
and then again in half.

519
00:30:31.371 --> 00:30:32.455
Can we go forever?

520
00:30:34.499 --> 00:30:38.545
And this is a question
that was debated since antiquity.

521
00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:41.381
[food processor whirring]

522
00:30:41.464 --> 00:30:48.346
Now, we have understood quite clearly
that a piece of rope is not continuous.

523
00:30:48.429 --> 00:30:51.140
That no piece of matter is continuous.

524
00:30:51.891 --> 00:30:57.313
It's made by little individual pieces
that we call molecules, atoms, particles.

525
00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:05.070
My job,

526
00:31:05.989 --> 00:31:07.031
as a physicist,

527
00:31:07.866 --> 00:31:12.996
is to study another kind of continuity,
which is a continuity...

528
00:31:13.079 --> 00:31:17.584
It's not of matter. String,
or a piece of wood, or a piece of metal.

529
00:31:17.667 --> 00:31:20.003
But the continuity of space itself.

530
00:31:21.004 --> 00:31:23.423
So just consider
the space between my hands

531
00:31:23.506 --> 00:31:26.092
and imagine to have it divide in half,

532
00:31:26.175 --> 00:31:28.136
and half, and half, and half.

533
00:31:29.679 --> 00:31:30.763
Can we go forever?

534
00:31:33.975 --> 00:31:36.144
[Brian] Is space truly continuous?

535
00:31:37.228 --> 00:31:39.480
Could it be infinitely divided?

536
00:31:41.941 --> 00:31:43.735
My gut feeling is that it can't.

537
00:31:44.277 --> 00:31:47.989
[Carlo] I think if we take
what we best know about the world,

538
00:31:48.072 --> 00:31:51.451
which is Einstein's General Relativity
Theory and quantum mechanics,

539
00:31:51.534 --> 00:31:53.077
and we bring them together,

540
00:31:53.703 --> 00:31:56.581
the clear consequence of that

541
00:31:57.165 --> 00:32:01.878
is that there's a minimal amount of space.
It's very small. Incredibly small.

542
00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:06.257
[Brian] Ten to the minus 33 centimeters,
the so-called Planck length.

543
00:32:06.925 --> 00:32:09.093
So incredibly tiny

544
00:32:09.177 --> 00:32:12.513
that it certainly stretches
the human imagination to...

545
00:32:14.474 --> 00:32:15.474
think about.

546
00:32:18.061 --> 00:32:19.061
So if you were to...

547
00:32:20.188 --> 00:32:21.397
take an individual atom

548
00:32:22.523 --> 00:32:27.195
and magnify it, expand it
to be as large as the observable universe,

549
00:32:27.278 --> 00:32:29.447
that's a huge scale of magnification.

550
00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:31.908
The Planck length under that magnification

551
00:32:31.991 --> 00:32:35.620
would grow to roughly be the size
of an average tree.

552
00:32:36.329 --> 00:32:41.793
So a tree is to the observable universe
as the Planck length is to an atom.

553
00:32:41.876 --> 00:32:43.795
So even on atomic scales,

554
00:32:44.587 --> 00:32:46.631
the distances that we're talking about

555
00:32:46.714 --> 00:32:50.426
where the notion of continuity
may break down,

556
00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:54.180
where discreteness may emerge,
they are fantastically small.

557
00:32:54.263 --> 00:32:56.265
[ethereal music plays]

558
00:32:58.601 --> 00:33:01.521
So I have a sort of, uh,

559
00:33:02.438 --> 00:33:07.068
pixelated vision of reality
at this small scale.

560
00:33:07.151 --> 00:33:11.406
It's like, uh,
if God didn't draw the universe

561
00:33:11.489 --> 00:33:14.784
with continuous lines
but just little pixels.

562
00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:21.290
It's many, many, many, many little things,
but it's not continuity.

563
00:33:21.374 --> 00:33:22.500
It's discreteness.

564
00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:24.752
It's finite.

565
00:33:25.837 --> 00:33:28.047
There is nothing infinitely small.

566
00:33:28.131 --> 00:33:29.465
[tense music plays]

567
00:33:35.388 --> 00:33:38.558
My favorite example
where we can find real infinities

568
00:33:38.641 --> 00:33:41.477
is when thinking about black holes.

569
00:33:41.561 --> 00:33:43.563
[tense music continues]

570
00:34:04.292 --> 00:34:06.794
Black holes are these massive objects

571
00:34:06.878 --> 00:34:09.797
where all the matter
is condensed so tightly

572
00:34:09.881 --> 00:34:12.925
that we get this region around it
called the event horizon,

573
00:34:13.009 --> 00:34:14.677
from which we get no information.

574
00:34:27.648 --> 00:34:30.735
[Janna] Now, there's nothing infinite
about the event horizon.

575
00:34:30.818 --> 00:34:33.821
It's actually empty.
A neutral region of space.

576
00:34:37.909 --> 00:34:40.286
You wouldn't even notice
when you cross the event horizon.

577
00:34:40.787 --> 00:34:43.539
You wouldn't feel anything weird happen
to your body.

578
00:34:43.623 --> 00:34:47.168
[Janna] What happens that's problematic
is on the inside of the black hole.

579
00:34:49.712 --> 00:34:51.714
[somber music plays]

580
00:35:01.808 --> 00:35:04.078
[Delilah] In the very literal sense,
we know nothing about

581
00:35:04.102 --> 00:35:06.312
what goes on inside of the black hole.

582
00:35:07.355 --> 00:35:09.357
But Einstein's theory
of general relativity

583
00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:12.401
says that, once you make it
past the event horizon,

584
00:35:13.528 --> 00:35:15.947
eventually if you keep just falling in...

585
00:35:18.407 --> 00:35:20.409
you'll reach a point called
the singularity.

586
00:35:21.786 --> 00:35:25.456
[Janna] A region which is
an infinite curvature in space-time.

587
00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:29.418
[Delilah] Where all the mass
of the black hole is concentrated.

588
00:35:29.502 --> 00:35:34.006
[Janna] Infinite densities,
and completely catastrophic.

589
00:35:34.841 --> 00:35:40.138
What happens that's really, um,
considered such a horror show

590
00:35:40.221 --> 00:35:42.640
is that, in a finite time,

591
00:35:43.766 --> 00:35:45.768
in microseconds, in fact,

592
00:35:45.852 --> 00:35:50.398
you would hit this region
of infinite curvature and infinite density

593
00:35:50.481 --> 00:35:52.817
and simply fall out of existence.

594
00:35:57.196 --> 00:35:58.698
[somber music ends]

595
00:35:58.781 --> 00:36:02.076
As though you weren't part of
the natural world anymore,

596
00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:03.828
so you weren't... Physics stopped.

597
00:36:07.582 --> 00:36:09.250
It's really a violation

598
00:36:09.333 --> 00:36:11.836
of the whole continuity of the program

599
00:36:12.795 --> 00:36:14.380
of understanding nature.

600
00:36:14.463 --> 00:36:16.966
It starts to say
nature's fundamentally unknowable

601
00:36:17.049 --> 00:36:19.552
in this one secret place
inside this black hole.

602
00:36:19.635 --> 00:36:21.262
And that just doesn't feel right.

603
00:36:23.389 --> 00:36:25.474
[Stephon] What do you do
when you have a theory

604
00:36:25.558 --> 00:36:26.684
that works really well

605
00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:29.937
except for when you have this infinity?

606
00:36:30.563 --> 00:36:32.899
Do you throw the baby out
with the bathwater,

607
00:36:32.982 --> 00:36:35.234
or is the infinity trying
to tell you something?

608
00:36:36.027 --> 00:36:38.446
The singularity,
this region of infinite curvature,

609
00:36:38.529 --> 00:36:41.449
infinite density,
I bet that doesn't really happen.

610
00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:46.162
[Stephon] The infinity
the equations predict is a hint.

611
00:36:46.871 --> 00:36:50.583
It hints at us,
"Hey, there's something new there."

612
00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:54.670
[Janna] Sometimes,
when I think about the infinity

613
00:36:54.712 --> 00:36:56.297
at the center of a black hole, like,

614
00:36:56.380 --> 00:36:58.716
I think about it as like a dying man

615
00:36:58.799 --> 00:37:02.428
scratching a clue in the dirt, telling us...

616
00:37:04.805 --> 00:37:07.141
"General relativity doesn't work here."

617
00:37:17.318 --> 00:37:18.569
I'm of the opinion

618
00:37:19.153 --> 00:37:23.950
that there is a new physics, actually,
in a black hole.

619
00:37:24.033 --> 00:37:26.035
[tense music plays]

620
00:37:28.329 --> 00:37:31.040
I think that one thing
that could be happening there

621
00:37:31.123 --> 00:37:33.709
is that there's some sort of portal...

622
00:37:35.336 --> 00:37:36.379
into a new realm.

623
00:37:36.462 --> 00:37:38.547
Maybe a new universe, for example.

624
00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:43.636
There's something I'm working on now

625
00:37:43.719 --> 00:37:47.098
that seems to show that that could happen
if the math works out.

626
00:37:50.726 --> 00:37:52.561
You could have certain portals

627
00:37:52.645 --> 00:37:54.855
where you can actually go through
the black hole.

628
00:37:58.442 --> 00:38:00.278
We call these things wormholes.

629
00:38:05.825 --> 00:38:07.576
[tense music intensifies]

630
00:38:11.914 --> 00:38:13.833
[Anthony] A wormhole is something where,

631
00:38:14.667 --> 00:38:18.045
at some point, I say, "Here's a sphere."

632
00:38:18.129 --> 00:38:22.174
"And I have a sphere inside this one
that's got a bigger area."

633
00:38:23.384 --> 00:38:24.384
Okay?

634
00:38:28.431 --> 00:38:32.351
And then inside that one is another sphere
with a bigger area than that.

635
00:38:38.107 --> 00:38:41.861
So the... At some level,
you know, we are confined by

636
00:38:41.944 --> 00:38:45.489
our normal understanding
of space and time, right?

637
00:38:45.573 --> 00:38:47.742
Something inside another thing is smaller.

638
00:38:50.578 --> 00:38:53.998
But that bigger thing is... in here.

639
00:38:54.081 --> 00:38:57.251
If I went in there,
I would be in that bigger thing.

640
00:38:57.960 --> 00:39:01.005
And yet I'm just seeing
the sort of outside of it.

641
00:39:03.007 --> 00:39:04.287
[producer] Do you understand it?

642
00:39:04.884 --> 00:39:07.345
Uh, it's totally clear mathematically,

643
00:39:07.428 --> 00:39:09.709
and when I look at this,
I have no idea how that could be.

644
00:39:09.764 --> 00:39:10.848
[chuckles]

645
00:39:11.849 --> 00:39:13.851
[tense music continues]

646
00:39:25.571 --> 00:39:26.571
Okay.

647
00:39:27.365 --> 00:39:29.909
[producer]
So I want you to think about this

648
00:39:29.992 --> 00:39:33.245
and tell me what it makes you
think about with infinity.

649
00:39:34.663 --> 00:39:35.664
Well, um,

650
00:39:36.749 --> 00:39:37.958
I would say that

651
00:39:38.959 --> 00:39:44.548
if I were some microscopic being
living at the surface of this... ball,

652
00:39:45.883 --> 00:39:48.010
I may conclude
that this surface is infinite,

653
00:39:48.677 --> 00:39:51.073
'cause it would take
an infinite amount of time, for example,

654
00:39:51.097 --> 00:39:53.265
in my imagination of infinity,

655
00:39:53.349 --> 00:39:55.935
to go from one side of the ball
to the other side.

656
00:39:56.435 --> 00:40:00.898
But, you know, it's not infinite, right?
For me, it's not infinite.

657
00:40:02.817 --> 00:40:05.528
[producer] So you're not holding infinity
in your hand?

658
00:40:06.904 --> 00:40:08.697
[laughs]

659
00:40:08.781 --> 00:40:11.158
Well, poetically, I am.

660
00:40:12.076 --> 00:40:13.994
I'm holding infinity in my hand.

661
00:40:14.954 --> 00:40:17.706
I see everything in this room
reflected in this sphere.

662
00:40:18.374 --> 00:40:19.875
If we didn't have walls in this room,

663
00:40:19.959 --> 00:40:23.003
I'd be able to see
the entire universe in this sphere.

664
00:40:23.087 --> 00:40:25.923
Well, the entire universe
that's behind me,

665
00:40:26.006 --> 00:40:31.512
because there's a path from everything
to this sphere and then to my eye.

666
00:40:33.472 --> 00:40:36.308
The one thing we cannot do in space-time

667
00:40:36.392 --> 00:40:38.936
is look down on the universe like this.

668
00:40:39.019 --> 00:40:42.481
And this is one of the,
in some terms, mistakes we make,

669
00:40:42.565 --> 00:40:45.151
uh, when we try to imagine
a finite universe.

670
00:40:45.234 --> 00:40:50.156
We imagine being in a space higher up,

671
00:40:50.239 --> 00:40:53.451
an extra dimension out,
and looking down on it.

672
00:40:53.534 --> 00:40:56.704
But, of course,
that would be part of the universe.

673
00:40:56.787 --> 00:40:59.790
If light could get to me,
that's part of the universe.

674
00:40:59.874 --> 00:41:01.792
So there is never such a thing.

675
00:41:01.876 --> 00:41:05.796
You can't jump out of the universe
and look down on it. Ever.

676
00:41:06.547 --> 00:41:08.632
[producer]
You're holding infinity. It's real.

677
00:41:09.967 --> 00:41:10.967
It's not.

678
00:41:11.510 --> 00:41:12.845
[Steven chuckles]

679
00:41:12.928 --> 00:41:16.307
Do you know the old story from Plato,
the prisoners in the cave?

680
00:41:16.390 --> 00:41:18.309
Right. So it's an old bit of philosophy,

681
00:41:18.392 --> 00:41:20.644
that the prisoners
are trapped in the cave,

682
00:41:20.728 --> 00:41:23.105
their backs are to the opening
of the cave,

683
00:41:24.023 --> 00:41:25.649
light is streaming into the cave,

684
00:41:25.733 --> 00:41:29.778
but all the prisoners can see
is shadows on the wall of the cave

685
00:41:29.862 --> 00:41:31.780
of things happening
out there in the world.

686
00:41:32.698 --> 00:41:35.451
To me, this is the shadow.

687
00:41:36.035 --> 00:41:39.497
This is not the real sphere.
This is not the perfect sphere.

688
00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:44.293
The perfect sphere would have infinitely
many points on its surface and inside.

689
00:41:44.877 --> 00:41:46.545
This thing is made of atoms.

690
00:41:47.296 --> 00:41:50.007
There's a lot of 'em,
but not infinitely many.

691
00:41:50.674 --> 00:41:52.218
It's a shadow of infinity.

692
00:41:54.553 --> 00:41:57.598
I love this shadow... [laughs]

693
00:41:57.681 --> 00:42:00.184
...because it gives me
a glimpse of infinity.

694
00:42:05.314 --> 00:42:07.316
[somber music plays]

695
00:42:11.278 --> 00:42:13.531
[Anthony] It... feels almost inconceivable.

696
00:42:16.742 --> 00:42:19.078
We're finite creatures,
we have access to finite things.

697
00:42:19.161 --> 00:42:21.372
We can only do so much in a lifetime.

698
00:42:22.498 --> 00:42:23.874
[static]

699
00:42:23.958 --> 00:42:26.627
Could we somehow nonetheless

700
00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:29.338
get a glimpse
into something that is truly infinite?

701
00:42:30.881 --> 00:42:32.967
And I think this is not impossible.

702
00:42:35.386 --> 00:42:38.430
An example that I've spent
quite a lot of time thinking about is,

703
00:42:38.514 --> 00:42:40.641
"What would happen to a physical system

704
00:42:40.724 --> 00:42:43.018
if you just wait
an infinite amount of time?"

705
00:42:48.649 --> 00:42:53.529
So let's imagine we take a box.
It's an excellent box.

706
00:42:54.029 --> 00:42:56.156
Nothing can come in. Nothing can go out.

707
00:42:57.408 --> 00:43:00.035
So we put an apple in the box,
close the box.

708
00:43:08.168 --> 00:43:09.503
We might come back in a month

709
00:43:09.587 --> 00:43:12.881
and the apple is looking
kind of mealy and decayed.

710
00:43:33.986 --> 00:43:36.280
Come back in a year,
the apple is a real mess.

711
00:43:36.905 --> 00:43:40.075
The apple is rotted.
Bacteria have done their thing.

712
00:43:51.045 --> 00:43:54.131
Come back in, you know, a hundred years...

713
00:43:57.384 --> 00:44:00.012
the apple is probably a kind of dust.

714
00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:04.266
The apple contains chemical energy,

715
00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:08.103
the same kind of energy you'd get
if you ate the apple or burned an apple.

716
00:44:08.187 --> 00:44:10.189
That energy will eventually come out,

717
00:44:10.898 --> 00:44:13.817
and so the apple inside the box
will get very hot,

718
00:44:13.901 --> 00:44:15.611
uh, probably thousands of degrees.

719
00:44:17.321 --> 00:44:20.157
Those particles can start
to nuclear-fuse together.

720
00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:21.825
[quiet thumps]

721
00:44:21.909 --> 00:44:23.285
This will take a really long time,

722
00:44:23.369 --> 00:44:26.789
because nuclear reactions happen
incredibly slowly at thousands of degrees,

723
00:44:26.872 --> 00:44:28.749
but eventually it will happen.

724
00:44:32.252 --> 00:44:37.341
Your apple has turned into millions
of degree plasma of fundamental particles

725
00:44:37.424 --> 00:44:39.885
and, you know,
burning into higher and higher things.

726
00:44:39.968 --> 00:44:43.514
Eventually, you'll end up
with probably some iron nuclei

727
00:44:43.597 --> 00:44:45.557
and lots of photons.

728
00:44:55.359 --> 00:44:57.361
[static]

729
00:45:01.156 --> 00:45:03.242
Billions and billions of years later...

730
00:45:07.830 --> 00:45:12.668
neutrons will decay into protons
and other fundamental particles,

731
00:45:13.627 --> 00:45:16.755
and then it'll sit there
for a very, very long time.

732
00:45:24.304 --> 00:45:27.307
Let's think about what the particles,
the protons and neutrons, and stuff.

733
00:45:27.391 --> 00:45:29.351
What do they...
How do they experience this?

734
00:45:30.686 --> 00:45:33.522
They are just cranking along,
obeying the laws of physics.

735
00:45:33.605 --> 00:45:37.151
The state of the box is changing
from one to the other, to the other.

736
00:45:37.651 --> 00:45:41.613
So, if there's 10 to the 24 particles
in an apple,

737
00:45:41.697 --> 00:45:46.660
there's something like
10 to the 10 to the 24 different states

738
00:45:46.744 --> 00:45:48.871
that those particles can be in.

739
00:45:50.873 --> 00:45:52.332
That's a gigantic number.

740
00:45:55.335 --> 00:45:56.587
But it isn't infinite.

741
00:45:57.296 --> 00:45:58.756
And what that means is that,

742
00:45:58.839 --> 00:46:01.675
if you let the box sit there
for an infinite amount of time,

743
00:46:01.759 --> 00:46:02.926
it will use them all up.

744
00:46:04.094 --> 00:46:06.722
It will go through
every possible state that it can,

745
00:46:06.805 --> 00:46:10.350
all 10 to the 10 to the 24
or whatever of them.

746
00:46:12.478 --> 00:46:14.897
And eventually,
it will start having to reuse

747
00:46:14.980 --> 00:46:16.690
states that it's been in before

748
00:46:16.774 --> 00:46:19.151
because there just aren't any more
that it can evolve into.

749
00:46:21.904 --> 00:46:25.032
And so, eventually,
if you wait a long time,

750
00:46:25.866 --> 00:46:27.117
something will happen.

751
00:46:27.618 --> 00:46:32.206
And this is the power
that infinity has over the finite.

752
00:46:34.708 --> 00:46:35.708
At some point,

753
00:46:36.668 --> 00:46:38.003
you could open the box

754
00:46:39.963 --> 00:46:41.673
and there's your apple again.

755
00:46:45.052 --> 00:46:49.973
How did that happen?
How did this hot gas turn into an apple?

756
00:46:50.057 --> 00:46:52.017
But eventually, it has to.

757
00:46:54.311 --> 00:46:59.441
In fact, every possible thing
that could exist in the box will exist.

758
00:46:59.525 --> 00:47:02.319
[fast motion sound]

759
00:47:02.402 --> 00:47:05.864
And they will each exist
an infinite number of times.

760
00:47:10.869 --> 00:47:13.038
[slow motion sound]

761
00:47:13.121 --> 00:47:14.498
Why do we care?

762
00:47:17.543 --> 00:47:20.462
Well, we might be in the box.

763
00:47:20.546 --> 00:47:22.548
[tense music plays]

764
00:47:24.299 --> 00:47:26.426
[Brian] In any finite region of space,

765
00:47:26.510 --> 00:47:29.471
like the observable universe
that we now inhabit,

766
00:47:29.555 --> 00:47:31.557
there's a finite amount of energy,

767
00:47:32.057 --> 00:47:34.852
which is carried
by a finite number of particles.

768
00:47:35.352 --> 00:47:37.020
And those finite number of particles

769
00:47:37.104 --> 00:47:41.483
can only be arranged
in finitely many distinct patterns.

770
00:47:41.567 --> 00:47:42.901
[tense music plays]

771
00:47:44.987 --> 00:47:47.406
Because there are only
finitely many distinct ways

772
00:47:47.489 --> 00:47:49.491
that the particles can be arranged,

773
00:47:49.575 --> 00:47:52.077
if space does go on infinitely far,

774
00:47:53.036 --> 00:47:55.664
the particle pattern
has to ultimately repeat.

775
00:47:55.747 --> 00:47:57.749
[tense music continues]

776
00:48:00.127 --> 00:48:03.422
And that would mean
there'd be copies of us out there.

777
00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:06.758
There'd be copies of us.

778
00:48:06.842 --> 00:48:09.177
- An infinite number of copies.
- Out there.

779
00:48:09.261 --> 00:48:11.179
- An infinite number of copies.
- Of us.

780
00:48:11.263 --> 00:48:13.974
Infinitely many of us
at very far parts of the universe

781
00:48:14.057 --> 00:48:16.685
doing exactly the same thing
that this copy of us is doing.

782
00:48:16.768 --> 00:48:20.397
Copies of me that continue,
and copies of me that die at any moment.

783
00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:23.775
Anything that can happen will happen
an infinite number of times.

784
00:48:23.859 --> 00:48:25.277
Suddenly, we're in a wild...

785
00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:27.988
- So somewhere there's an Earth...
- ...inhuman...

786
00:48:28.071 --> 00:48:30.991
<i>- ...where I'm having this conversation...</i>
- ...place.

787
00:48:31.074 --> 00:48:34.119
Where instead of being very hot right now,
it would be air-conditioned.

788
00:48:34.202 --> 00:48:36.204
An infinite number of copies where...

789
00:48:36.288 --> 00:48:39.082
An elephant suddenly appears
in front of me.

790
00:48:39.166 --> 00:48:40.792
- [laughs]
- There could be

791
00:48:40.876 --> 00:48:43.086
our exact universe,
but with a different history.

792
00:48:43.170 --> 00:48:45.881
<i>Hillary Clinton won the election.
Germany won the war.</i>

793
00:48:45.964 --> 00:48:49.051
And on that Earth,
the dinosaurs might still rule.

794
00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:53.221
[Anthony] I think when a lot of people
hear some of these ideas,

795
00:48:53.305 --> 00:48:57.768
they're like, "God, they were just up late
drinking and having a good time

796
00:48:57.851 --> 00:48:59.311
and came up with this crazy idea

797
00:48:59.394 --> 00:49:01.688
'cause they wanted to think it
in science fiction."

798
00:49:01.772 --> 00:49:02.772
[roars]

799
00:49:03.774 --> 00:49:07.069
But this is reality-based. [laughs]

800
00:49:07.152 --> 00:49:09.696
[Brian] If the universe is infinite
in extent,

801
00:49:09.780 --> 00:49:13.283
there would be an infinite number
of Einsteins in our universe.

802
00:49:13.784 --> 00:49:15.619
And some of them would be talking to you

803
00:49:15.702 --> 00:49:18.413
and probably be giving much better answers
that I'm giving.

804
00:49:18.914 --> 00:49:21.833
[Einstein echoing] According to
the general theory of relativity,

805
00:49:22.501 --> 00:49:27.047
it is probable
that the universe is not infinite

806
00:49:27.839 --> 00:49:30.384
but closed in upon itself.

807
00:49:30.968 --> 00:49:34.721
Something like the surface of a sphere.

808
00:49:35.305 --> 00:49:38.433
One thing I have learned in a long life...

809
00:49:38.517 --> 00:49:40.227
[Brian] But those other Einsteins,

810
00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:43.021
they're very far away.

811
00:49:43.105 --> 00:49:45.148
[Einstein talks indistinctly]

812
00:49:45.232 --> 00:49:47.901
[Brian] We may never be
in contact with them,

813
00:49:47.985 --> 00:49:51.113
because, according
to his theory of relativity,

814
00:49:51.822 --> 00:49:57.369
nothing can go faster
than 186,000 miles per second,

815
00:49:57.452 --> 00:49:59.121
which is the speed of light.

816
00:50:01.623 --> 00:50:03.625
[mellow music plays]

817
00:50:06.336 --> 00:50:10.757
We have this view of reality that...

818
00:50:10.841 --> 00:50:13.301
We look at it in front of us, right?

819
00:50:13.385 --> 00:50:15.512
I mean, I see you,

820
00:50:15.595 --> 00:50:17.764
I see that white screen,

821
00:50:17.848 --> 00:50:21.768
I see the camera, as they are now, right?

822
00:50:21.852 --> 00:50:26.314
Which means if I see them
as all you are now,

823
00:50:26.398 --> 00:50:27.899
I see immediately.

824
00:50:27.983 --> 00:50:31.570
So the light
that comes from all these objects to me

825
00:50:31.653 --> 00:50:34.781
arrives instantaneously at infinite speed.

826
00:50:35.449 --> 00:50:41.163
Once upon a time, we did think that
perhaps things happened instantaneously

827
00:50:41.246 --> 00:50:45.250
and that information
between one point and another

828
00:50:45.333 --> 00:50:48.920
could be conveyed in the blink of an eye,
in a single instance.

829
00:50:49.004 --> 00:50:52.716
Well, no, of course.
It doesn't come at infinite speed.

830
00:50:52.799 --> 00:50:54.801
It takes some time to come.

831
00:50:54.885 --> 00:50:58.305
So I don't see the now.
I see the past in reality.

832
00:50:58.388 --> 00:51:00.724
Why? Because there's no infinite speed.

833
00:51:05.771 --> 00:51:07.898
[jaunty jazz music plays]

834
00:51:15.906 --> 00:51:18.408
[announcer]
<i>Ladies and gentlemen, there he is.</i>

835
00:51:18.492 --> 00:51:24.498
<i>The whizzingest wave,
the peppiest particle. Philo T. Photon!</i>

836
00:51:26.083 --> 00:51:27.542
<i>Move over, Magellan.</i>

837
00:51:27.626 --> 00:51:31.922
<i>Philo is primed to perform
his most fabulous feat of derring-do yet.</i>

838
00:51:32.005 --> 00:51:36.802
<i>Circumnavigating the globe
eight times in a single second.</i>

839
00:51:36.885 --> 00:51:37.928
[crowd cheers]

840
00:51:38.011 --> 00:51:39.888
<i>On your mark, Philo.</i>

841
00:51:39.971 --> 00:51:41.973
[old Western music plays]

842
00:51:52.984 --> 00:51:54.569
[crowd cheering]

843
00:51:55.487 --> 00:51:58.824
<i>He did it, ladies and gentlemen.
The son of a gun did it!</i>

844
00:51:58.907 --> 00:52:00.283
Oh...

845
00:52:00.367 --> 00:52:01.660
[crowd cheers and applauds]

846
00:52:02.994 --> 00:52:05.038
<i>Let's see that again in real-time.</i>

847
00:52:08.750 --> 00:52:10.168
Do it again! Encore!

848
00:52:10.252 --> 00:52:12.838
<i>And now I'm sure Philo is pooped.</i>

849
00:52:13.463 --> 00:52:14.463
<i>What's this?</i>

850
00:52:15.048 --> 00:52:18.468
<i>He's going to fly
to the next galaxy and back?</i>

851
00:52:19.219 --> 00:52:22.514
<i>No one has ever attempted
a stunt like this before.</i>

852
00:52:22.597 --> 00:52:25.100
<i>Ladies and gentlemen, I can't watch!</i>

853
00:52:25.183 --> 00:52:27.894
[jaunty jazz music plays]

854
00:52:48.498 --> 00:52:51.084
[Delilah] The speed of light is
the maximum speed

855
00:52:51.168 --> 00:52:53.795
at which anything can travel in the
universe.

856
00:52:53.879 --> 00:52:55.881
[somber music plays]

857
00:53:00.927 --> 00:53:02.179
[clock ticks]

858
00:53:02.262 --> 00:53:04.222
[Carlo] The speed of light, it's...

859
00:53:05.098 --> 00:53:06.892
it's incredibly fast.

860
00:53:09.519 --> 00:53:13.273
And the speed of light
is horrendously slow.

861
00:53:16.318 --> 00:53:21.114
If we wanna travel in the galaxy,
it's hard enough, uh,

862
00:53:21.740 --> 00:53:25.243
but if you want to travel in the universe
from galaxy to galaxy,

863
00:53:26.870 --> 00:53:29.623
we just cannot, because we cannot...
We're too slow.

864
00:53:30.207 --> 00:53:33.001
And light is too slow. Incredibly slow.

865
00:53:33.877 --> 00:53:35.879
[somber music continues]

866
00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:42.761
[Delilah] So is the universe infinite?

867
00:53:48.600 --> 00:53:51.144
[Carlo] It's hard to think
what is the universe.

868
00:53:54.564 --> 00:53:59.736
We know that we see the universe
as many billion light years,

869
00:54:00.403 --> 00:54:03.615
uh, and we know
the universe is larger than what we see.

870
00:54:03.698 --> 00:54:05.909
We have indications of that.

871
00:54:05.992 --> 00:54:10.413
But it could be maybe ten times larger.
Maybe a hundred times larger.

872
00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:16.378
There are parts of the universe
most probably

873
00:54:16.461 --> 00:54:20.257
in which, even if we send a signal now,
it will never get there.

874
00:54:23.343 --> 00:54:27.055
So the universe is very,
very, very, very, very big.

875
00:54:27.138 --> 00:54:30.016
And it makes our heads spin.

876
00:54:39.109 --> 00:54:41.111
[somber music continues]

877
00:54:56.626 --> 00:54:58.461
[somber music ends]

878
00:54:58.545 --> 00:55:00.547
[piano music plays]

879
00:55:04.426 --> 00:55:06.761
[Alan]
I was looking up at the sky one night

880
00:55:07.929 --> 00:55:09.764
when I was about ten years old.

881
00:55:12.392 --> 00:55:13.392
And...

882
00:55:17.314 --> 00:55:20.817
I felt like my life didn't matter.

883
00:55:29.242 --> 00:55:33.997
And I guess it was converting
large space to large time.

884
00:55:37.584 --> 00:55:40.337
One star after another star
after another star...

885
00:55:40.420 --> 00:55:42.088
[water dripping]

886
00:55:43.214 --> 00:55:45.759
...and wondering whether
that would keep going...

887
00:55:47.635 --> 00:55:49.637
[wind chimes tinkling]

888
00:55:49.721 --> 00:55:50.721
...forever.

889
00:55:56.394 --> 00:56:01.232
I had this sense that the universe,
it existed a long time before I was born...

890
00:56:03.693 --> 00:56:06.613
and it would exist
a long time after I was dead.

891
00:56:10.658 --> 00:56:14.829
And I was just a speck...
that didn't matter.

892
00:56:24.339 --> 00:56:27.092
I don't matter. My parents don't matter.

893
00:56:28.885 --> 00:56:30.261
Nothing matters.

894
00:56:34.307 --> 00:56:36.351
We're all just specks.

895
00:56:36.434 --> 00:56:38.895
We're just living in this brief moment.

896
00:56:41.689 --> 00:56:45.443
None of us were here a million years ago.

897
00:56:47.612 --> 00:56:50.323
None of us will be here
a million years from now.

898
00:56:53.910 --> 00:56:55.703
And the universe doesn't care.

899
00:56:58.790 --> 00:57:00.750
It just goes on and on and on.

900
00:57:05.380 --> 00:57:06.380
So...

901
00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:09.384
why are we wasting time,

902
00:57:09.467 --> 00:57:12.679
you know, going to school,
having dentist appointments?

903
00:57:14.180 --> 00:57:15.180
All of that.

904
00:57:15.765 --> 00:57:18.810
Why are we wasting our time?
Because none of it matters.

905
00:57:25.775 --> 00:57:26.775
And then...

906
00:57:31.364 --> 00:57:33.241
[birds singing]

907
00:57:33.324 --> 00:57:34.367
...I fell in love.

908
00:57:38.079 --> 00:57:39.747
And that changed everything.

909
00:57:44.419 --> 00:57:47.005
That mattered. [laughs]

910
00:57:47.088 --> 00:57:50.967
Even though we might both
be specks in the cosmos.

911
00:57:55.180 --> 00:57:57.182
[mellow music plays]

912
00:58:04.147 --> 00:58:10.403
[Carlo] One of the oldest questions
that thinkers puzzled on

913
00:58:10.487 --> 00:58:15.241
is what happens in the universe
if I could fly forever?

914
00:58:15.325 --> 00:58:16.826
What would happen?

915
00:58:20.205 --> 00:58:23.124
In the past, it seemed
that there were two alternatives.

916
00:58:23.208 --> 00:58:25.543
Both too strange.

917
00:58:27.462 --> 00:58:30.965
One is that the universe is infinite
and I could go forever.

918
00:58:34.761 --> 00:58:37.597
And the other is that it's finite
and there is a wall.

919
00:58:39.474 --> 00:58:41.059
But if there is a wall,

920
00:58:41.768 --> 00:58:44.854
then I could go through the wall,

921
00:58:45.730 --> 00:58:47.106
and so what's next?

922
00:58:50.193 --> 00:58:52.904
And Einstein published
this spectacular paper.

923
00:58:52.987 --> 00:58:57.200
He said, "No, no, no.
The universe can be finite,

924
00:58:58.535 --> 00:59:01.746
but without walls,
because if I go in one direction,

925
00:59:01.829 --> 00:59:04.624
I keep going, and I come back
from the other direction."

926
00:59:06.251 --> 00:59:07.627
Like it happens on the Earth.

927
00:59:07.710 --> 00:59:10.421
If I walk on the Earth towards east,

928
00:59:10.505 --> 00:59:13.216
I walk, walk, walk, walk.
Do I find a wall? No.

929
00:59:13.716 --> 00:59:17.679
Is Earth infinite? No. What happened?
I just come back from the other side.

930
00:59:17.762 --> 00:59:19.764
[somber music plays]

931
00:59:30.191 --> 00:59:32.277
And it's very possible today

932
00:59:33.403 --> 00:59:35.822
that the universe in fact has this shape.

933
00:59:38.199 --> 00:59:42.537
Namely, has no boundary, there's no wall
at the end of the universe,

934
00:59:42.620 --> 00:59:43.830
but it's finite.

935
00:59:46.291 --> 00:59:47.834
Huge, but finite.

936
00:59:51.004 --> 00:59:55.341
[Rebecca] There's actually way more ways
to make the universe finite,

937
00:59:56.092 --> 01:00:00.346
just geometrically, abstractly playing
around with whatever you want,

938
01:00:00.430 --> 01:00:02.307
than there are to make it infinite.

939
01:00:04.767 --> 01:00:10.148
In fact, there's an infinite number
of ways to make the universe finite,

940
01:00:10.231 --> 01:00:13.026
and there's really only a couple of ways
to make it infinite.

941
01:00:13.109 --> 01:00:15.111
[upbeat music plays]

942
01:00:18.323 --> 01:00:21.784
[Delilah] We do know
that the universe could be infinite,

943
01:00:22.452 --> 01:00:28.124
but we don't actually have a mechanism
to measure an infinite amount of length.

944
01:00:30.918 --> 01:00:34.922
And because light travels
at a finite speed

945
01:00:35.006 --> 01:00:37.050
and the age of the universe is finite,

946
01:00:37.133 --> 01:00:41.471
there will only ever be
a finite subset of the universe

947
01:00:41.554 --> 01:00:43.431
that we'll ever be able to observe.

948
01:00:44.474 --> 01:00:46.851
Whether beyond that
it's finite or infinite,

949
01:00:46.934 --> 01:00:49.062
we just will never
be able to experimentally know.

950
01:00:49.812 --> 01:00:51.189
[upbeat music ends]

951
01:00:55.777 --> 01:00:57.403
When we talk about infinity,

952
01:00:57.487 --> 01:01:03.326
we can certainly talk about infinity
in the extent of the spatial domain.

953
01:01:03.910 --> 01:01:07.330
We can also talk about infinity
in the temporal domain.

954
01:01:07.413 --> 01:01:09.832
The duration of the universe.

955
01:01:09.916 --> 01:01:13.086
It's possible that the universe
will continue on expanding

956
01:01:13.586 --> 01:01:16.881
into an infinite time in the future.

957
01:01:16.964 --> 01:01:19.676
That's what
the equations predict, actually.

958
01:01:27.058 --> 01:01:28.309
[Brian] In 1998,

959
01:01:29.644 --> 01:01:35.400
we discovered that the universe
is not only expanding,

960
01:01:35.483 --> 01:01:39.195
but the rate of expansion is accelerating.

961
01:01:41.614 --> 01:01:44.534
[tense music plays]

962
01:01:44.617 --> 01:01:48.538
The galaxies are moving away
from each other with increasing speed.

963
01:01:54.001 --> 01:01:57.046
And what this means

964
01:01:57.130 --> 01:02:01.759
is that, eventually, we will be cut off

965
01:02:02.969 --> 01:02:04.721
from other galaxies.

966
01:02:13.813 --> 01:02:19.694
And so, as the stars in our galaxy
eventually burn out...

967
01:02:22.321 --> 01:02:23.531
as they will,

968
01:02:24.490 --> 01:02:27.785
'cause all of them
have a limited amount of nuclear fuel...

969
01:02:31.330 --> 01:02:37.003
there eventually will not be
any new energy sources in our galaxy.

970
01:02:46.262 --> 01:02:48.264
[ethereal music plays]

971
01:03:11.370 --> 01:03:16.417
And since we will be cut off
from all other galaxies eventually,

972
01:03:16.501 --> 01:03:19.212
maybe about 100 billion years from now...

973
01:03:22.256 --> 01:03:25.426
there will not be
any energy sources at all

974
01:03:27.094 --> 01:03:31.015
and life will completely...

975
01:03:33.100 --> 01:03:34.101
end.

976
01:03:39.148 --> 01:03:43.236
And so, roughly 100 billion years
from now...

977
01:03:45.655 --> 01:03:47.365
that will be the end of life.

978
01:03:52.703 --> 01:03:56.666
And if we keep on going along
the cosmological timeline,

979
01:03:57.834 --> 01:03:59.961
galaxies, planets, black holes,

980
01:04:00.044 --> 01:04:03.381
everything that we know about,
it will all disintegrate.

981
01:04:10.888 --> 01:04:14.433
So all that would be left at that point

982
01:04:14.517 --> 01:04:19.105
are particles wafting
through the darkness.

983
01:04:19.188 --> 01:04:21.190
[wind howling]

984
01:04:30.366 --> 01:04:32.368
[tense music plays]

985
01:04:35.288 --> 01:04:39.417
Now, if the universe exists
in an infinite amount of time,

986
01:04:39.500 --> 01:04:41.377
as we now think that it will,

987
01:04:43.129 --> 01:04:48.217
the era of life is just a sliver of time

988
01:04:48.301 --> 01:04:52.805
when you look at the full unfolding
of the universe and time.

989
01:04:59.562 --> 01:05:02.982
Even though 100 billion years
seems like a long time,

990
01:05:05.693 --> 01:05:07.778
it's nothing compared to infinity.

991
01:05:22.084 --> 01:05:26.213
[Janna] Even if human beings make it
past this particular... [laughs]

992
01:05:26.297 --> 01:05:30.760
...immediate crisis,
or other sentient life emerges,

993
01:05:32.303 --> 01:05:34.722
there will be a last sentient being.

994
01:05:34.805 --> 01:05:37.016
There will be a last living creature.

995
01:05:40.269 --> 01:05:42.313
Even if the universe is infinite,

996
01:05:43.648 --> 01:05:46.275
there will be a last thought.

997
01:06:12.051 --> 01:06:17.723
[Delilah] A lot of people have kind of
this visceral anxiety about not existing.

998
01:06:20.351 --> 01:06:22.031
But, personally,
I don't have this feeling.

999
01:06:22.103 --> 01:06:25.439
I'm like, you know,
once I didn't exist, before I was born.

1000
01:06:25.523 --> 01:06:29.694
There will be a point in time
when I don't exist because I'll die.

1001
01:06:33.698 --> 01:06:35.992
And the same is true for our species.

1002
01:06:40.121 --> 01:06:41.414
And all life.

1003
01:06:59.849 --> 01:07:03.936
[drum music plays]

1004
01:07:04.020 --> 01:07:05.020
Um,

1005
01:07:05.438 --> 01:07:10.359
It's, it's... You know,
even physicists have feelings. [laughs]

1006
01:07:10.443 --> 01:07:12.987
And so we can ask,
"How does that make you feel?"

1007
01:07:13.070 --> 01:07:16.741
"Are you...
As a physicist, is it daunting?"

1008
01:07:16.824 --> 01:07:19.994
"Is it, you know, an existential crisis?"

1009
01:07:20.077 --> 01:07:22.329
I... I have never...

1010
01:07:22.413 --> 01:07:24.165
I've always had the opposite reaction,

1011
01:07:24.248 --> 01:07:28.044
even to some of
the most apocalyptic predictions

1012
01:07:28.127 --> 01:07:29.587
for the future of the universe.

1013
01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:35.092
It gives me a great sense
of meaning and connectedness

1014
01:07:35.176 --> 01:07:39.221
to appreciate that you're part
of this grand picture.

1015
01:07:39.305 --> 01:07:42.725
Nothing is permanent in that sense.

1016
01:07:42.808 --> 01:07:46.353
And to... to my mind, that's freeing.

1017
01:07:47.021 --> 01:07:49.899
It frees us
from this focus on the permanent

1018
01:07:49.982 --> 01:07:52.568
as the place
where value ultimately resides,

1019
01:07:53.277 --> 01:07:58.282
to a focus
on the brief moment that we have,

1020
01:07:58.365 --> 01:08:02.453
in which we can understand things,
and create beauty,

1021
01:08:02.995 --> 01:08:08.584
and experience wonder, regardless
of how fleeting that experience may be.

1022
01:08:08.667 --> 01:08:12.379
That the universe itself
gets to have its window of life,

1023
01:08:12.463 --> 01:08:15.883
its window of consciousness
and beauty and love,

1024
01:08:15.966 --> 01:08:17.843
and then, poof.

1025
01:08:19.303 --> 01:08:22.431
It's sort of like the universe as a whole
is living the way we do.

1026
01:08:23.891 --> 01:08:27.061
We're only here for a short time
compared to infinity,

1027
01:08:27.978 --> 01:08:30.106
and, to me, that's a holy thought.

1028
01:08:30.189 --> 01:08:33.859
I mean, that's...
as close as I can get to being religious.

1029
01:08:33.943 --> 01:08:38.114
The gift that you have of consciousness
for the short time that you're here.

1030
01:08:38.197 --> 01:08:40.199
[majestic music plays]

1031
01:08:44.161 --> 01:08:46.163
[majestic music ends]

1032
01:08:59.009 --> 01:09:01.554
Wait, what was I trying to say?
Something popped into my head.

1033
01:09:08.936 --> 01:09:10.938
[eerie music plays]

1034
01:09:16.485 --> 01:09:19.321
[Eugenia] There are interesting concepts
in abstract math

1035
01:09:19.405 --> 01:09:24.243
where you can take something
that appears to have one characteristic,

1036
01:09:24.326 --> 01:09:27.788
but you can put it inside something else
or put it around something else

1037
01:09:27.872 --> 01:09:30.749
and it will look different
from that point of view.

1038
01:09:30.833 --> 01:09:34.920
And, in a way, I think that the universe

1039
01:09:35.796 --> 01:09:40.885
is infinite in some sense,
compared with our own lives,

1040
01:09:40.968 --> 01:09:42.636
because we just won't be there.

1041
01:09:42.720 --> 01:09:44.388
We won't be there to see it.

1042
01:09:45.723 --> 01:09:48.017
Whether it's really infinite or not,

1043
01:09:48.559 --> 01:09:50.603
I don't know,
and I think that's wonderful.

1044
01:09:53.564 --> 01:09:55.691
[Delilah] Not knowing doesn't make me sad.

1045
01:09:57.526 --> 01:10:00.779
As a scientist,
for me, not knowing is exciting.

1046
01:10:01.989 --> 01:10:05.659
[Stephon] There are things that I believe
that our minds can't know,

1047
01:10:05.743 --> 01:10:07.953
but they are real and they exist.

1048
01:10:09.914 --> 01:10:13.125
If we want to call that the infinite,
if we want to call that spirit,

1049
01:10:13.209 --> 01:10:16.503
if we want to call that God,
whatever you want to call that thing,

1050
01:10:16.587 --> 01:10:19.423
I believe that thing is for real,

1051
01:10:20.758 --> 01:10:21.759
but not knowable.

1052
01:10:21.842 --> 01:10:24.220
[crickets chirping]

1053
01:10:24.303 --> 01:10:26.388
Infinity's very large.
It's too big, infinity.

1054
01:10:28.849 --> 01:10:34.355
To me, infinity is an emotion that we get
in front of the immensity of nature.

1055
01:10:36.649 --> 01:10:38.484
We are little teeny things.

1056
01:10:39.485 --> 01:10:42.696
We do science. It's great.
I love to do science.

1057
01:10:42.780 --> 01:10:45.366
Try to figure out
the quantum properties of gravity,

1058
01:10:45.449 --> 01:10:47.826
trying to figure out
what is the shape of the cosmos.

1059
01:10:48.327 --> 01:10:49.787
But the reality is

1060
01:10:51.372 --> 01:10:53.749
we are, you know, like a little cat

1061
01:10:53.832 --> 01:10:55.793
trying to understand quantum mechanics.

1062
01:10:56.835 --> 01:10:58.963
The cat isn't going
to understand quantum mechanics

1063
01:10:59.046 --> 01:11:01.173
because he has the brain it has.

1064
01:11:01.257 --> 01:11:04.426
A poor human like me won't understand
everything about the universe

1065
01:11:04.510 --> 01:11:06.720
because of the poor brain I have.

1066
01:11:14.728 --> 01:11:16.397
The number of neurons in our brain

1067
01:11:16.480 --> 01:11:18.899
is comparable to the number of stars
in the galaxies.

1068
01:11:18.983 --> 01:11:20.067
It is immense.

1069
01:11:23.237 --> 01:11:27.700
Which means that the space
of thinkable thoughts

1070
01:11:27.783 --> 01:11:30.786
is a fantastically big number.

1071
01:11:30.869 --> 01:11:33.831
It's a one followed by billions of digits.

1072
01:11:34.581 --> 01:11:36.667
It's an incredibly large number.

1073
01:11:37.584 --> 01:11:40.045
Larger than anything we have encountered.

1074
01:11:42.089 --> 01:11:44.717
But, look, our brain,

1075
01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:47.052
it's a kilogram of meat

1076
01:11:47.678 --> 01:11:51.598
which can be in one configuration
or the other, and that's it.

1077
01:11:51.682 --> 01:11:54.268
Uh, you can list all of them in principle.

1078
01:11:55.477 --> 01:11:58.105
Once again, there's no Infinity here,

1079
01:11:59.148 --> 01:12:03.277
and we have to confront
the fundamental finiteness,

1080
01:12:03.360 --> 01:12:05.529
not of nature, but of ourselves.

1081
01:12:05.612 --> 01:12:11.160
[girl] <i>One, two, three, four, five...</i>

1082
01:12:11.243 --> 01:12:14.121
[producer] Do you think
that human creativity is infinite?

1083
01:12:14.788 --> 01:12:18.334
- [girl] <i>...eight, nine, ten...</i>
- [laughs] Uh...

1084
01:12:18.417 --> 01:12:21.503
[girl] <i>...eleven, twelve, thirteen...</i>

1085
01:12:21.587 --> 01:12:24.048
I sort of recoil at that suggestion.

1086
01:12:24.131 --> 01:12:26.884
I don't know. Oh, good gracious. I...

1087
01:12:28.010 --> 01:12:31.430
I don't know.
I don't know that we are infinite.

1088
01:12:31.513 --> 01:12:36.977
[girl] <i>Twenty-one, twenty-two,
twenty-three. Seven, eight, nine,</i>

1089
01:12:37.644 --> 01:12:41.315
<i>ten, eleven, twelve.</i>

1090
01:12:41.398 --> 01:12:48.322
<i>Ninety-one, ninety-two, ninety-three,
ninety-four, ninety-five, ninety-six...</i>

1091
01:12:48.405 --> 01:12:50.407
[girl continues to count indistinctly]

1092
01:12:55.704 --> 01:12:59.124
[Steven] For some reason, thinking about
anything about human beings being infinite

1093
01:12:59.208 --> 01:13:00.709
doesn't seem right to me.

1094
01:13:01.794 --> 01:13:03.962
We feel very bounded, to me.

1095
01:13:04.046 --> 01:13:07.674
Bounded in our rationality,
bounded in our creativity.

1096
01:13:07.758 --> 01:13:12.679
[girl continues to count indistinctly]

1097
01:13:15.182 --> 01:13:18.727
[Delilah] Yeah, I don't even really know
how to visualize infinity.

1098
01:13:18.811 --> 01:13:21.271
[girl] <i>One, two, three...</i>

1099
01:13:21.355 --> 01:13:23.148
[Kenny] Can I even picture a thousand?

1100
01:13:23.232 --> 01:13:25.150
[girl] <i>...nine, five, two...</i>

1101
01:13:25.234 --> 01:13:28.695
[Kenny] Even that number
is too big for me to properly imagine.

1102
01:13:28.779 --> 01:13:31.698
[girl continues to count indistinctly]

1103
01:13:33.325 --> 01:13:34.993
I find that...

1104
01:13:35.077 --> 01:13:38.205
it's hard for me to really imagine
numbers bigger than ten.

1105
01:13:38.288 --> 01:13:40.290
[upbeat music plays]

1106
01:13:44.670 --> 01:13:47.881
<i>♪ Is it real or fantasy? ♪</i>

1107
01:13:49.800 --> 01:13:51.969
<i>♪ Is it just a game we play ♪</i>

1108
01:13:52.052 --> 01:13:58.183
<i>♪ To pass the time while slips away
In front of me ♪</i>

1109
01:13:59.935 --> 01:14:05.065
<i>♪ Possibilities to find
As we walk the line ♪</i>

1110
01:14:05.149 --> 01:14:09.903
<i>♪ Faster and slower
And over and over ♪</i>

1111
01:14:09.987 --> 01:14:13.907
<i>♪ We tumble and we intertwine ♪</i>

1112
01:14:15.284 --> 01:14:21.832
<i>♪ Solving the riddle
And little by little we find ♪</i>

1113
01:14:21.915 --> 01:14:25.461
<i>♪ It goes on and on ♪</i>

1114
01:14:25.544 --> 01:14:30.799
<i>♪ Forever and ever and ever and ever ♪</i>

1115
01:14:30.883 --> 01:14:35.888
<i>♪ Forever and ever and ever and ever ♪</i>

1116
01:14:35.971 --> 01:14:40.851
<i>♪ Forever and ever and ever and ever ♪</i>

1117
01:14:40.934 --> 01:14:46.231
<i>♪ Forever and ever and ever and ever ♪</i>

1118
01:14:46.315 --> 01:14:49.776
<i>♪ Love it's all that matters in the end ♪</i>

1119
01:14:49.860 --> 01:14:53.989
<i>♪ At the end of forever ♪</i>

1120
01:14:56.450 --> 01:14:59.411
<i>♪ Space is staring back at me ♪</i>

1121
01:15:01.747 --> 01:15:06.668
<i>♪ It's not easy to explain
Where all just links in the daisy chain ♪</i>

1122
01:15:06.752 --> 01:15:10.380
<i>♪ Will you recognize just what you see? ♪</i>

1123
01:15:12.007 --> 01:15:14.259
<i>♪ Search and you won't miss the signs ♪</i>

1124
01:15:14.343 --> 01:15:17.095
<i>♪ The ones the universe left behind ♪</i>

1125
01:15:17.179 --> 01:15:21.850
<i>♪ From the beginning
We're spinning and spinning ♪</i>

1126
01:15:21.934 --> 01:15:26.396
<i>♪ And spiraling out of control ♪</i>

1127
01:15:27.397 --> 01:15:29.399
<i>♪ It's never-ending ♪</i>

1128
01:15:29.483 --> 01:15:33.946
<i>♪ We never know where we will go ♪</i>

1129
01:15:34.029 --> 01:15:37.449
<i>♪ It's goes on and on ♪</i>

1130
01:15:37.533 --> 01:15:42.663
<i>♪ Forever and ever and ever and ever ♪</i>

1131
